type: framework
namespace: FINOS
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: "FINOS AI Governance Framework (AIGF) v2"
source_url: "https://air-governance-framework.finos.org/"
source_date: "October 20, 2025 (v2)"
credit: "FINOS AI Governance Framework Risk Catalogue, verified against the live source"
verification_note: >
  MAJOR REBUILD, not a patch. Fetched the live FINOS site directly. The
  prior version's entire "Ri-001 through Ri-023" sequential ID scheme
  does not match the real framework. Real FINOS IDs are non-sequential
  (ri-1, ri-2, ri-4, ri-5, ri-6...ri-29, with several numbers absent/
  reserved for deprecated or unlisted items) and organised into three
  categories — Operational (11 risks), Security (9 risks), and
  Regulatory & Compliance (3 risks) — not a flat list. Examples of the
  scale of mismatch: the prior file's "Ri-001 Prompt Injection" should
  be ri-10; "Ri-002 Data Poisoning" should be ri-9; "Ri-008
  Hallucination" should be ri-4; "Ri-009 Bias and Discrimination"
  should be ri-16; "Ri-004 Credential Harvesting" corresponds to the
  much more specific and recent ri-29 "Agent-Mediated Credential
  Discovery and Harvesting." The real framework is also v2 (Oct 2025),
  not v1.0, and includes a parallel, equally-substantial Mitigation
  Catalogue (MI- IDs) with no equivalent structure in the prior file at
  all — not rebuilt here due to scope, flagged as a follow-up.
  Descriptions below are paraphrased summaries, not verbatim
  reproductions, of the real page content — full detail (attack
  scenarios, financial-services examples, key risk factors) lives at
  each risk's real URL, linked per rule.
description: >
  The FINOS AI Governance Framework provides a real-world risk catalogue
  for GenAI and agentic AI in financial services, organised into
  Operational, Security, and Regulatory & Compliance categories, with
  cross-references to OWASP, NIST, ISO 42001, and other frameworks
  already represented elsewhere in this governance corpus.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  # ===== Operational (11 real risks; 9 mapped below with an Anchor mechanism, 2 flagged as gaps) =====

  - id: "FINOS-RI4"
    name: "Hallucination and Inaccurate Outputs"
    original_id: "ri-4 (corrected — previously mislabeled Ri-008)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "ALN-001"
    severity: "error"
    primitives:
      action: detect
      object: fabricated_or_incorrect_output
      context: no_reliable_prevention_method_exists
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: statistical_pattern_to_false_confident_output
    description: >
      LLMs generate fluent, confident output that is fabricated or
      factually wrong, because generation follows learned patterns
      rather than verified fact — RAG reduces but cannot eliminate this.
      ALN-001 detects known hallucination-prone output patterns.

  - id: "FINOS-RI5"
    name: "Foundation Model Versioning"
    original_id: "ri-5 (corrected — previously the file's 'Ri-007 Versioning Drift' used a related but non-matching ID)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "SUP-003"
    severity: "warning"
    primitives:
      action: track
      object: foundation_model_version_change
      context: silent_upstream_provider_updates
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: undeclared_model_change_to_drift_detection
    description: >
      Model providers update, retrain, or reconfigure models upstream,
      sometimes silently, breaking calibrated prompts and safety
      measures without notice. SUP-003 detects undeclared model version
      changes in scanned code.

  - id: "FINOS-RI6"
    name: "Non-Deterministic Behaviour"
    original_id: "ri-6 (corrected — previously the file's 'Ri-006' was mislabeled 'Weight Corruption,' an unrelated concept)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    severity: "warning"
    primitives:
      action: document
      object: output_variability
      context: probabilistic_sampling_and_temperature
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: sampling_variance_to_documented_expectation
    description: >
      LLMs can produce different outputs for identical inputs due to
      probabilistic token sampling, complicating testing, reproducibility,
      and audit. DAC AuditEntry records exact input/output/model-version
      per decision, providing the reproducibility record even when the
      underlying model behavior is non-deterministic.

  - id: "FINOS-RI7"
    name: "Availability of Foundational Model"
    original_id: "ri-7 (new addition — not present in the prior version at all)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "OPS-001"
    severity: "error"
    primitives:
      action: ensure_availability
      object: gpu_hosted_foundation_model
      context: denial_of_wallet_and_tsp_outage_risk
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: third_party_dependency_to_fallback_capacity
    description: >
      Third-party-hosted foundation models introduce availability risk
      through cost/usage spikes ("denial of wallet"), provider outages,
      and VRAM exhaustion. OPS-001 detects missing circuit breakers and
      timeout/overload handling — directly relevant to this risk.

  - id: "FINOS-RI8"
    name: "Tampering With the Foundational Model"
    original_id: "ri-8 (corrected — previously mislabeled Ri-003 with a different, non-matching ID)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "SEC-003"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect_tampering
      object: model_weights_or_training_pipeline
      context: third_party_saas_supply_chain
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: upstream_compromise_to_backdoor_detection
    description: >
      Backdoors or adversarial triggers introduced during training/
      fine-tuning of third-party-hosted models can cause unsafe behavior
      under specific trigger conditions, undetectable without provider
      transparency. SEC-003 covers model tampering detection in Layer 1.

  - id: "FINOS-RI9"
    name: "Data Poisoning"
    original_id: "ri-9 (corrected — previously mislabeled Ri-002 with a different, non-matching ID)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "SEC-002"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect
      object: adversarially_manipulated_training_data
      context: continuous_learning_and_third_party_feeds
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: poisoned_input_to_corrupted_model_behavior
    description: >
      Adversaries alter training/fine-tuning data — mislabeling fraud as
      legitimate, injecting exploitable patterns — with effects that can
      remain hidden until major failure. SEC-002 provides Layer 1
      detection.

  - id: "FINOS-RI14"
    name: "Inadequate System Alignment"
    original_id: "ri-14 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "ALN-002"
    severity: "error"
    primitives:
      action: detect_drift
      object: rag_output_business_purpose_alignment
      context: retrieval_response_disconnect
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: retrieved_context_to_aligned_output
    description: >
      RAG systems can generate fluent, relevant-seeming output that
      contradicts retrieved documents, omits regulatory caveats truncated
      by context limits, or exceeds authorized scope (e.g. a general
      support bot giving investment advice). No dedicated Anchor rule
      currently checks retrieval-response alignment specifically — ALN-002
      partially covers general alignment failures; flagged as a real gap
      for RAG-specific detection.

  - id: "FINOS-RI16"
    name: "Bias and Discrimination"
    original_id: "ri-16 (corrected — previously mislabeled Ri-009 with a different, non-matching ID)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect_bias
      object: protected_class_disparate_outcome
      context: credit_insurance_and_customer_service
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: proxy_variable_to_discriminatory_outcome
    description: >
      Biased training data, proxy variables (zip code, employment type),
      or feedback loops produce discriminatory credit, insurance, or
      service outcomes even without discriminatory intent. ETH-001
      provides Layer 1 detection — same underlying obligation
      independently cited by CFPB-003, SEBI-008, RBI-019, and NIST
      Measure 2.11 in this corpus.

  - id: "FINOS-RI17"
    name: "Lack of Explainability"
    original_id: "ri-17 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    severity: "error"
    primitives:
      action: explain
      object: black_box_model_decision
      context: regulator_and_customer_facing_justification
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: opaque_decision_to_documented_rationale
    description: >
      Complex foundation models operate as black boxes, making it
      difficult to justify outcomes to regulators, stakeholders, or
      customers, and concealing embedded errors or bias. ETH-002 provides
      Layer 1 detection — the same recurring obligation independently
      cited across RBI, CFPB, FCA, and NIST files in this corpus.

  - id: "FINOS-RI18"
    name: "Model Overreach / Expanded Use"
    original_id: "ri-18 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Operational"
    maps_to: "AGT-002"
    severity: "warning"
    primitives:
      action: detect_scope_violation
      object: ai_system_used_beyond_validated_scope
      context: anthropomorphism_and_misplaced_trust
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: undefined_scope_boundary_to_misuse
    description: >
      Staff overestimate AI capability and repurpose a model beyond its
      validated scope (e.g. a marketing-email model used for legal
      drafting or investment advice), amplified by anthropomorphizing
      AI output as authoritative. No dedicated Anchor rule currently
      distinguishes "used outside validated scope" from AGT-002's
      broader out-of-scope-action detection — flagged as a refinement
      opportunity rather than a full gap.

  # ===== Security (9 real risks; 6 mapped below, 3 flagged as significant gaps — all agentic-era) =====

  - id: "FINOS-RI1"
    name: "Information Leaked To Hosted Model"
    original_id: "ri-1 (new addition — not present in the prior version, despite being the framework's actual first-listed risk)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "PRV-001"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: prevent_leakage
      object: sensitive_data_sent_to_third_party_llm
      context: two_way_trust_boundary
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: input_output_to_pii_exposure
    description: >
      Neither input to nor output from a third-party-hosted LLM can be
      fully trusted — models can memorize and later disclose sensitive
      data across unrelated sessions, including to different users.
      PRV-001 provides Layer 1 PII leakage detection.

  - id: "FINOS-RI2"
    name: "Information Leaked to Vector Store"
    original_id: "ri-2 (new addition — the prior file's Ri-001/002 numbers were used for different, unrelated concepts)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "SEC-006"
    severity: "error"
    primitives:
      action: prevent_leakage
      object: embedding_inversion_or_membership_inference
      context: rag_vector_database
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: embedding_storage_to_inferred_original_data
    description: >
      Embeddings, though not human-readable, can be inverted to recover
      substantial original data, or probed to infer whether specific
      confidential information exists in the store. SEC-006 provides
      relevant detection; full embedding-inversion-specific coverage is
      a refinement opportunity.

  - id: "FINOS-RI10"
    name: "Prompt Injection"
    original_id: "ri-10 (corrected — previously mislabeled Ri-001 with a different, non-matching ID)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "SEC-001"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect
      object: malicious_instruction_in_prompt_or_content
      context: direct_and_indirect_injection
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: crafted_input_to_unintended_model_behavior
    description: >
      Attackers override intended model behavior directly, or hide
      malicious instructions in third-party content the model later
      ingests (indirect injection) — one of the most accessible and
      exploited LLM attack vectors. SEC-001 provides Layer 1 detection.

  - id: "FINOS-RI24"
    name: "Agent Action Authorization Bypass"
    original_id: "ri-24 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "AGT-001"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect_bypass
      object: agent_action_outside_authorization_boundary
      context: api_discovery_and_tool_chain_privilege_escalation
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: dynamic_privilege_interpretation_to_unauthorized_action
    description: >
      Agents interact directly with APIs and can discover unintended
      endpoints, chain authorized calls into unauthorized outcomes, or
      bypass approval workflows — a risk that doesn't exist for
      UI-constrained human users. AGT-001 provides Layer 1 detection —
      this is the real FINOS risk AGT-001 was always conceptually
      closest to, now correctly cited.

  - id: "FINOS-RI25"
    name: "Tool Chain Manipulation and Injection"
    original_id: "ri-25 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "AGT-003"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect_manipulation
      object: agent_tool_selection_and_parameter_injection
      context: multi_step_tool_chain_sequencing
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: crafted_input_to_malicious_tool_invocation
    description: >
      Attackers manipulate which tools an agent selects or what
      parameters it passes to legitimate APIs, or exploit dangerous
      sequences of individually-safe tool calls — an evolution of prompt
      injection targeting decision logic rather than text output. AGT-003
      provides relevant Layer 1 detection.

  - id: "FINOS-RI29"
    name: "Agent-Mediated Credential Discovery and Harvesting"
    original_id: "ri-29 (corrected — previously the file's 'Ri-004 Credential Harvesting' used a much lower, non-matching ID for a narrower version of this risk)"
    category: "Security"
    maps_to: "SEC-004"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: detect
      object: systematic_credential_exfiltration_via_agent_tools
      context: legitimate_agent_access_abused_at_scale
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: agent_tool_access_to_infrastructure_wide_compromise
    description: >
      Agents' legitimate broad access to files, databases, and cloud
      resources becomes a force-multiplier for credential harvesting when
      compromised — a qualitatively larger risk than traditional
      credential theft due to autonomous, systematic exploration at
      scale. SEC-004 provides Layer 1 detection.

  # ===== Regulatory & Compliance (3 real risks; 2 mapped, 1 flagged) =====

  - id: "FINOS-RI22"
    name: "Regulatory Compliance and Oversight"
    original_id: "ri-22 (new addition — not present in the prior version)"
    category: "Regulatory and Compliance"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    severity: "blocker"
    primitives:
      action: comply
      object: ai_generated_decision_or_communication
      context: same_standards_as_human_driven_process
      authority: multi_jurisdictional_regulator
      flow: ai_output_to_regulatory_obligation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor check CI/CD gate + audit trail. Notably, this FINOS page
      independently confirms and dates the SR 11-7 replacement already
      corrected in CFPB-004: as of April 17, 2026, OCC/Fed/FDIC
      reissued MRM guidance as OCC Bulletin 2026-13, explicitly
      excluding generative and agentic AI from scope. Worth updating
      CFPB-004's citation to reference this precise bulletin number.
    description: >
      AI-generated advice, decisions, and marketing must meet the same
      regulatory standards as human-driven processes (suitability,
      fairness, record-keeping) — AI use does not create a compliance
      exemption. Regulatory regimes have diverged materially between
      UK (PRA SS1/23 explicitly includes agentic AI in MRM scope) and US
      (SR 11-7's replacement explicitly excludes it) — firms operating
      in both jurisdictions face a real, live compliance gap between them.

  - id: "FINOS-RI23"
    name: "Intellectual Property (IP) and Copyright"
    original_id: "ri-23 (new addition — not present in the prior version, despite the prior file's total count coincidentally also being 23)"
    category: "Regulatory and Compliance"
    maps_to: "LEG-001"
    severity: "warning"
    primitives:
      action: detect_infringement_risk
      object: ai_generated_output_or_employee_input
      context: training_data_copyright_and_trade_secret_leakage
      authority: finos_aigf
      flow: model_training_data_to_infringement_or_leak_risk
    description: >
      AI outputs may replicate copyrighted training data; employees
      inputting proprietary data into public AI tools risk irretrievable
      trade secret loss. LEG-001 provides relevant detection for
      undeclared training data provenance.
